# Chapter 2 · The Suffrage Movement: How Women Won the Vote

> "**Suffragist movement**" was a short note in ==2019==, and MCQs asked what suffrage means (**the right to vote**, 2019) and which country first gave women the vote (**New Zealand**, 2024). This chapter covers the movement in the United States, Britain, the wider world and South Asia.

## 2.1  What suffrage means

**Suffrage** is the **right to vote** in political elections. **Women's suffrage** is women's right to vote on the same terms as men. Until the late nineteenth century, almost no country allowed women to vote in national elections. The **suffrage movement** — or **suffragist movement** — was the organised campaign, lasting about eighty years in the West, to win that right. It was the central demand of **first-wave feminism**.

::: fig compare | Suffragists and suffragettes.
## Suffragists
- Campaigners for votes for women in general
- Peaceful, lawful methods: petitions, meetings, lobbying, pamphlets
- Britain: **National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies** (NUWSS, ==1897==), led by **Millicent Fawcett**
- Larger in numbers; argued from reason and justice
## Suffragettes
- A name the *Daily Mail* gave in ==1906== to militant British campaigners
- Direct action: heckling, marches, window-smashing, arson, hunger strikes
- Britain: **Women's Social and Political Union** (WSPU, ==1903==), led by **Emmeline Pankhurst** and her daughters
- Motto: "**Deeds, not words**"
:::

## 2.2  Why women were denied the vote

In the nineteenth century, voting was tied to **property**, **independence** and the "public sphere". The arguments against women's votes were:

- **Separate spheres:** women belonged in the home; politics was men's world.
- **Coverture:** a married woman's legal identity was absorbed into her husband's, so he "represented" her.
- **Nature:** women were said to be too emotional or weak for political judgement.
- **Social order:** votes for women would divide families and weaken the state.

The suffragists answered with the language of **natural rights** (women are human beings with equal reason), **democracy** (no taxation without representation), and **social improvement** (women would bring morality, temperance and care to politics).

## 2.3  The United States

::: fig timeline | The American suffrage movement.
@layout vertical
1840: Stanton and Mott refused seats at the World Anti-Slavery Convention, London
1848: Seneca Falls Convention — Declaration of Sentiments demands the vote
1869: Movement splits — National Woman Suffrage Association (Stanton, Anthony) and American Woman Suffrage Association (Stone)
1869: Wyoming Territory gives women the vote
1872: Susan B. Anthony arrested for voting
1890: The two unite as NAWSA
1913: Alice Paul's suffrage parade in Washington
1917: "Silent Sentinels" picket the White House; many jailed
1920: 19th Amendment — women's vote nationwide
:::

The movement began at the **Seneca Falls Convention** (19–20 July ==1848==), organised by **Elizabeth Cady Stanton** and **Lucretia Mott**. Its **Declaration of Sentiments** declared that "all men and women are created equal" and demanded the vote — the most controversial resolution, supported by the abolitionist **Frederick Douglass**.

After the Civil War, the **15th Amendment** (==1870==) gave the vote to Black men but not to women, splitting the movement. **Susan B. Anthony** and Stanton formed the **National Woman Suffrage Association** (==1869==), seeking a federal amendment; **Lucy Stone** formed the **American Woman Suffrage Association**, working state by state. They merged in ==1890== as the **National American Woman Suffrage Association** (NAWSA). Western states — **Wyoming** (==1869==), Utah, Colorado, Idaho — gave women the vote first.

In the 1910s, **Alice Paul**'s more militant National Woman's Party picketed the White House; arrested pickets went on hunger strike and were force-fed. Women's war work in the **First World War** strengthened their case. The **19th Amendment** (ratified ==1920==) declared that the right to vote shall not be denied "on account of sex". Many Black women in the South, however, remained barred by discriminatory laws until the **Voting Rights Act** of ==1965==.

## 2.4  Britain

::: fig timeline | The British suffrage movement.
@layout vertical
1792: Mary Wollstonecraft, A Vindication of the Rights of Woman
1866: J. S. Mill presents a women's suffrage petition to Parliament
1869: Mill, The Subjection of Women; single women ratepayers vote in local elections
1897: NUWSS formed under Millicent Fawcett
1903: WSPU formed by Emmeline Pankhurst
1913: "Cat and Mouse Act"; Emily Wilding Davison dies at the Epsom Derby
1918: Representation of the People Act — women over 30 with property qualifications vote
1928: Equal Franchise Act — women vote at 21, as men do
:::

In ==1866== **John Stuart Mill**, then an MP, presented a petition for women's suffrage and in ==1867== proposed amending the Reform Bill to replace "man" with "person"; it failed. His book *The Subjection of Women* (==1869==) became a classic of liberal feminism.

The **suffragists** of the **NUWSS** (==1897==), led by **Millicent Fawcett**, used patient, lawful pressure. Frustrated by slow progress, **Emmeline Pankhurst** founded the **WSPU** (==1903==). Its **suffragettes** heckled ministers, chained themselves to railings, smashed windows and set fire to post boxes. Imprisoned suffragettes went on **hunger strike**; the government force-fed them, then passed the **Prisoners (Temporary Discharge for Ill-Health) Act** (==1913==) — the "**Cat and Mouse Act**" — releasing them when weak and re-arresting them when recovered. In ==1913== **Emily Wilding Davison** ran onto the course at the Epsom Derby and was struck by the King's horse; she died days later.

During the **First World War** (1914–18) both groups suspended campaigning and supported the war effort, while women worked in munitions factories and services. The **Representation of the People Act** (==1918==) gave the vote to women over **30** who met property qualifications (about 8.4 million women). The **Equal Franchise Act** (==1928==) gave women the vote at **21**, on equal terms with men.

## 2.5  The wider world

::: fig timeline | When women won the vote — selected countries.
@layout vertical
1893: New Zealand — first self-governing country (Kate Sheppard's petitions)
1902: Australia (federal, but not Aboriginal women until 1962)
1906: Finland — first in Europe, and first to let women stand for parliament
1913: Norway
1917: Russia (after the revolution)
1918: Germany, Britain (over 30), Poland
1920: United States
1931: Sri Lanka (Ceylon) — universal adult franchise, among the first in Asia
1944: France
1947: Pakistan and India at independence
1971: Switzerland (federal level)
2015: Saudi Arabia (municipal elections)
:::

**New Zealand** (==1893==) was the first self-governing country to give all women the vote, after petitions organised by **Kate Sheppard** gathered about 32,000 signatures. **Finland** (==1906==) was the first to let women both vote and stand; 19 women entered its parliament in 1907. Women in many countries won the vote after the **First World War**, and in France and Italy after the **Second**. In much of Asia and Africa women gained the vote with **independence** from colonial rule, often at the same time as men.

## 2.5a  Suffrage in the Muslim world

Women's suffrage came to Muslim-majority countries at different times, usually with modern statehood.

- **Azerbaijan** (==1918==) is often cited as the first Muslim-majority country to give women the vote, during its short-lived democratic republic.
- **Turkey** gave women the vote in local elections in ==1930== and national elections in ==1934==, under Atatürk's reforms; eighteen women entered parliament in 1935.
- **Pakistan** (==1947==), **Indonesia** (==1945==) and **Bangladesh** (==1971==) began with adult franchise at independence.
- **Iran** gave women the vote in ==1963==; **Kuwait** in ==2005==; **Saudi Arabia** in municipal elections in ==2015==.

These dates show that the Muslim world is not uniform: many Muslim-majority countries gave women the vote **before** Switzerland (==1971==). Today Muslim-majority states such as **Pakistan**, **Bangladesh**, **Indonesia** and **Tunisia** use gender quotas, and Pakistan, Bangladesh, Turkey and Indonesia have had women heads of government.

## 2.6  Suffrage in South Asia

In colonial India, women's political rights grew step by step.

1. **1917:** The **Women's Indian Association** (Annie Besant, Margaret Cousins, Dorothy Jinarajadasa) and **Sarojini Naidu** led a delegation to the Secretary of State, Edwin Montagu, demanding the vote on the same terms as men.
2. **1919:** The Government of India Act left the question to provincial legislatures. **Madras** (==1921==) became the first province to enfranchise women, on property qualifications; others followed.
3. **1935:** The **Government of India Act** extended the vote to about **six million** women and **reserved seats** for women in the legislatures — the origin of reserved seats in the subcontinent.
4. **1937–46:** Women contested elections; the **All-India Muslim League** formed a women's sub-committee in ==1938==. Women such as **Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz** and **Begum Shaista Ikramullah** became leaders.
5. **1947:** Pakistan began with **adult franchise** for women and men. The first **Constituent Assembly** had **two** women — Jahanara Shahnawaz and Shaista Ikramullah (MCQs 2016, 2024).

::: pakistan | Women voters in Pakistan's early years
Women voted in Pakistan's provincial elections from the start. The **1956 Constitution** confirmed universal adult franchise, and the first direct nationwide general election, in ==1970==, was held on it. In the ==1965== presidential election, **Fatima Jinnah** challenged Ayub Khan — the first woman to contest for the country's highest office. The voting age was lowered from 21 to **18** in ==2002== (MCQ 2016).
:::

## 2.6a  The ideas behind the movement — justice and expediency

The suffragists used two families of argument, and historians still debate which mattered more.

1. **Justice (equal rights) arguments:** women are rational human beings with the same natural rights as men. **Mary Wollstonecraft** (*A Vindication of the Rights of Woman*, ==1792==) argued that women seem inferior only because they are denied education; the **Declaration of Sentiments** (==1848==) rewrote the Declaration of Independence to say that "all men **and women** are created equal"; **John Stuart Mill** (*The Subjection of Women*, ==1869==) argued that the legal subordination of women was wrong in itself and a barrier to human progress.
2. **Expediency (difference) arguments:** women would **improve** politics because of their distinct qualities — as mothers and moral guardians they would support temperance, child welfare, public health and peace. These arguments, common in the later movement, reassured opponents that votes for women would not overturn the family.

::: balance | Which argument won the vote?
- **Justice** gave the movement its principle and its link to democracy.
- **Expediency** — together with women's **war work** in 1914–18 — persuaded many reluctant politicians.
-> **Line to take:** the vote was won by a **combination** of principle, practical argument, organisation (suffragists) and pressure (suffragettes), at a moment when war had changed ideas of citizenship.
:::

## 2.7  Achievements and limits of the suffrage movement

::: balance | Assessing the suffrage movement
- **Achievements:** won the vote and the right to stand in most of the world; trained generations of women in organising; linked feminism to democracy; opened the way to education, property and legal reforms.
- **Limits:** many suffragists were white, middle-class and sometimes racist or class-biased (Black women in the US South; working-class women in Britain under the 1918 property rule); the vote did not end discrimination in law, work or the family — which led to the **second wave**; formal rights did not ensure real participation.
-> **Line to take:** suffrage was a necessary but not sufficient step. Pakistan shows this clearly — women have voted since 1947, but in 2024 there were still ==9.94 million== fewer women than men on the electoral roll.
:::

::: eye | The model structure for the 2019 short note
1. Meaning: suffrage = the right to vote; suffragist vs suffragette. 2. Origins: Wollstonecraft, Seneca Falls (1848). 3. United States: NWSA/AWSA 1869, NAWSA 1890, 19th Amendment 1920. 4. Britain: Mill 1866, NUWSS 1897, WSPU 1903, 1918 and 1928. 5. The world: New Zealand 1893, Finland 1906. 6. South Asia: 1917 delegation, Madras 1921, 1935 Act, Pakistan 1947. 7. Assessment: necessary but not sufficient. Draw the world timeline.
:::
